---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Tag from "../../components/ui/Tag.astro";

const stack = ["Python", "BigQuery", "Google Sheets API", "LLM Agent", "SAP"];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="This AI Catches Underperforming New Product Launches Before SCM Has To"
  description="A pipeline that pulls demand data from BigQuery (sourced from SAP), fills in a Google Sheet using the Supply Chain team's own formula, and lets anyone ask an AI bot which new product launches are falling behind."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-28">
    <p class="label-mono mb-4">Minor project</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-medium tracking-tight">
      This AI Catches Underperforming New Product Launches Before SCM Has To
    </h1>

    <p class="text-lg text-ink-muted mt-6 leading-relaxed">
      Every new product launch (NPD, short for New Product Development) needs its demand forecast calculated, then
      tracked against real sales for its first two months on shelf. The Supply Chain (SCM) team used to do this by
      hand in Excel — pull the numbers, apply the formula, update the sheet, repeat for every SKU, every month, with
      dozens of launches running at once. I automated the pipeline end to end: a script pulls the source data from
      BigQuery (sourced from SAP) and fills in the demand numbers straight into a Google Sheet using SCM's own
      formula. Then, because dozens of live launches means dozens of rows to scan for the ones going wrong, I
      connected an AI bot that can just be asked — in plain language — which ones are underperforming right now.
    </p>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-1.5 mt-6">
      {stack.map((t) => <Tag>{t}</Tag>)}
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 p-4 rounded-xl border border-danger/40 bg-danger-soft">
      <p class="label-mono text-danger mb-2">⚠ Sensitive data notice</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
        Every screenshot below is from the real, live system — but product names, Material IDs, and product codes
        are internal SAP identifiers tied to a live, confidential catalog, and have been blocked out before
        publishing. What's left visible is the calculation logic and demand figures, shown to explain how the
        system works, not to disclose any specific product's numbers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="label-mono mt-16 mb-4">Act 1 — from formula to spreadsheet, automatically</p>
    <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
      The sheet tracks every new product from its initial demand estimate (<span class="font-mono text-ink">Estimasi
      NPD</span>) through its first two active months on shelf. Product identity — name, brand, Material ID, product
      code, location — sits on the left; the redacted blocks below are exactly where that identity would be.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-border bg-surface">
      <img
        src="/demo/npd-automation/sheet-left.png"
        alt="Automated Google Sheet showing NPD demand-tracking columns, with product name, brand, and Material ID redacted"
        class="w-full h-auto"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint mt-2">
      Left side of the sheet: product identity (redacted) and the initial demand estimate, filled in automatically.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mt-8">
      Scroll right on the same sheet and the identity columns are gone — this half is pure tracking. Actual demand
      in month one (<span class="font-mono text-ink">Demand M1</span>) is compared against the original estimate as
      a percentage; the same happens again in month two against a repeat-purchase target SCM sets. A <span class="font-mono text-ink">Status</span> column — <span class="font-mono text-ink">PRE-M1</span>, <span class="font-mono text-ink">M1 AKTIF</span>, <span class="font-mono text-ink">M2 AKTIF</span>, or <span class="font-mono text-ink">SO Belum Terdeteksi</span> ("sales
      order not yet detected") — is derived automatically too, so no one has to remember which stage each of
      forty-plus launches is in.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-border bg-surface">
      <img
        src="/demo/npd-automation/sheet-right.png"
        alt="Right side of the same Google Sheet showing Demand M1, Demand M2, percent-vs-estimate columns, and an automatically derived lifecycle Status column"
        class="w-full h-auto"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint mt-2">
      Right side: month-1 and month-2 actuals vs. estimate, and the auto-derived lifecycle status per product.
    </p>

    <p class="label-mono mt-16 mb-4">Act 2 — ask it directly, in plain language</p>
    <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
      An accurate sheet still isn't the same as an answer. With forty-plus launches live at once, someone still has
      to scroll the whole thing to find which ones are actually struggling. So the same data sits behind a chat bot:
      ask it a question, and it filters, aggregates, and ranks the sheet itself instead of a person doing it by eye.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-border bg-surface max-w-md mx-auto">
      <img
        src="/demo/npd-automation/bot-analysis.png"
        alt="Chat bot response analyzing the 5 worst-performing completed products by month-1 demand, with product identities redacted"
        class="w-full h-auto"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint mt-2 text-center">
      Asked for the 5 worst SELESAI (completed) products — the bot filters, ranks, and summarizes on its own.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mt-8">
      That single answer is doing more than a lookup: it filtered forty products down to only the completed ones,
      ranked them by month-1 performance, named the worst performer, and then flagged a pattern a quick glance would
      miss — 28 of those "completed" products still show month-2 demand above 100% of estimate, meaning a finished
      status doesn't mean repeat demand tapered off. That's the kind of cross-check SCM used to have to go looking
      for.
    </p>

    <p class="label-mono mt-16 mb-6">Business impact</p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-3">
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface">
        <p class="font-mono text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-accent">No manual Excel</p>
        <p class="label-mono mt-1">Formula runs itself, monthly</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface">
        <p class="font-mono text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-accent">Same formula, every time</p>
        <p class="label-mono mt-1">No copy-paste or transcription slips</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface">
        <p class="font-mono text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-ink">Ask, don't scroll</p>
        <p class="label-mono mt-1">Scales past 40+ live launches</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 rounded-xl border border-border border-l-4 border-l-accent bg-surface">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-9 h-9 rounded-lg bg-accent-soft text-accent flex items-center justify-center">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" />
            <polyline points="12 7 12 12 15.5 13.5" />
          </svg>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
          <span class="text-ink font-medium">Gives SCM their time back.</span> No one has to hand-update a formula
          across dozens of rows every month anymore — the sheet fills itself in on schedule, straight from source
          data.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 rounded-xl border border-border border-l-4 border-l-accent bg-surface">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-9 h-9 rounded-lg bg-accent-soft text-accent flex items-center justify-center">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M12 3l7 3v6c0 4.5-3 8-7 9-4-1-7-4.5-7-9V6l7-3Z" />
            <polyline points="9 12 11 14 15 9" />
          </svg>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
          <span class="text-ink font-medium">Makes the calculation more accurate, not just faster.</span> The exact
          formula SCM specified runs the same way on every row, every month — no manual transcription error, no
          version of the sheet quietly drifting from the agreed method.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex gap-4 p-4 rounded-xl border border-border border-l-4 border-l-warn bg-surface">
        <div class="shrink-0 w-9 h-9 rounded-lg bg-warn-soft text-warn flex items-center justify-center">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M4 4h16v12H9l-5 4V4Z" />
            <line x1="8" y1="9" x2="16" y2="9" />
            <line x1="8" y1="12.5" x2="13" y2="12.5" />
          </svg>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">
          <span class="text-ink font-medium">Keeps working as launch volume grows.</span> A bigger NPD pipeline used
          to mean more rows for someone to scan by eye. Now it just means more rows the bot can filter — the
          question stays just as fast to ask.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <details class="mt-16 group">
      <summary class="label-mono cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center gap-2">
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform group-open:rotate-90">›</span>
        How it works (technical)
      </summary>
      <div class="text-ink-muted space-y-4 leading-relaxed mt-6 text-sm">
        <p>
          A scheduled script queries BigQuery — which mirrors the underlying SAP data — for each new product's sales
          orders, then computes the estimate and month-1/month-2 actuals using the formula SCM specified, and writes
          the results into the tracking sheet through the Google Sheets API. The lifecycle <span class="font-mono text-ink">Status</span> is
          derived from the same write, not set by hand: it's a function of which dates and sales-order records are
          present for that row on a given run.
        </p>
        <p>
          The chat layer reads the same sheet rather than a separate copy of the data, so it's never out of sync
          with what a human would see by opening it directly. A query like "5 worst products, SELESAI status only"
          gets parsed into a filter and an aggregation over that data — not a canned report — which is what lets it
          answer a follow-up like the M2-over-100% pattern without that being a report anyone had to think to build
          in advance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
